  + SQL Server writes a plain row limit as TOP n. With an offset or a UNION it uses OFFSET … ROWS FETCH NEXT n ROWS ONLY, which needs ORDER BY, so the builder adds ORDER BY (SELECT NULL) when there is none."
`;

exports[`explanation text for every example > filter-sort in sqlserver: beginner 1`] = `
"Returns Name and Salary from Employees, where Salary is greater than 50000, sorted by Salary descending, first 10 rows only.
FROM: Picks where the rows come from.
WHERE: Keeps only the rows that match, before any grouping.
SELECT: Works out the output columns.
ORDER BY: Sorts the final result.
TOP: Returns only the first rows of the sorted result."
`;

exports[`explanation text for every example > filter-sort in sqlserver: developer 1`] = `
"Returns Name and Salary from Employees, where Salary is greater than 50000, sorted by Salary descending, first 10 rows only.
FROM: Picks where the rows come from.
WHERE: Keeps only the rows that match, before any grouping.
  + A comparison with NULL is neither true nor false, so rows with NULL in a compared column are left out; IS NULL finds them.
  + WHERE can't use aggregates such as COUNT or SUM; those filters go in HAVING.
SELECT: Works out the output columns.
ORDER BY: Sorts the final result.
  + Rows with equal sort values can come back in any order; a unique column (such as id) as the last sort key makes the order stable.
TOP: Returns only the first rows of the sorted result.
  + The limit is applied after sorting, so it keeps the first rows of the sorted result."
`;

exports[`explanation text for every example > insert-rows in generic: advanced 1`] = `
"Adds 2 rows to employees, filling name, department and salary.
VALUES: The rows you typed, one new row each.
  + Columns that aren't listed get their default value, or NULL.
INSERT INTO: Adds the rows to the table.
  + If any row breaks a constraint (a unique key, NOT NULL or a foreign key), the statement fails and no rows are added."
`;

