exports[`explanation text for every example > page-results in postgresql: advanced 1`] = `
"Returns id, name and email from customers, sorted by name then id, rows 41 to 60.
FROM: Picks where the rows come from.
SELECT: Works out the output columns.
ORDER BY: Sorts the final result.
  + Rows with equal sort values can come back in any order; a unique column (such as id) as the last sort key makes the order stable.
  + PostgreSQL sorts NULLs last in ascending order and first in descending order; NULLS FIRST or NULLS LAST changes that.
LIMIT: Returns only the first rows of the sorted result.
  + The limit is applied after sorting, so it keeps the first rows of the sorted result.
  + PostgreSQL applies LIMIT and OFFSET after sorting. OFFSET still reads the rows it skips, so later pages read more rows."
`;

